  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon features broad northwesterly flow \nacross the CONUS, with slight ridging propping that flow pattern up \nfrom the Pacific Coast. Taking a look at the surface, we find \nourselves in the the path of a northward-advancing warm front that \nhas helped power temperatures into the upper 70s to low 80s already. \nHigh temperature guidance continues along the under-predictive \ntrend as of late, which has longer-term bias correction hurting \nthe forecast. Later this evening, the low-level jet is expected \nto ramp up as the warm front lifts northward, resulting in a \nnarrow band of rain chances (15-25%) that will skirt across the \nnortheastern forecast area. Total amounts should only be a trace\nto a couple of hundredths, with most staying dry. Along and \njust north of that front, we could see some areas of fog develop\nacross northeast into north-central Nebraska, with visibilities\ndropping below 1 mile tomorrow morning after 4 AM (20% chance).\nAnother warm day is forecast for tomorrow as southerly winds \nreturn, with no additional rain chances and highs in the mid- \nto-upper 80s. Gusts of 20-25 mph will be creeping in from the \nwest, and will be strongest across northeast and north-central \nNebraska. \n\nSaturday and Beyond:\n\nOur hottest day of the forecast is on tap for Saturday as a ridge to \nthe west progresses quickly to the east through the area and we \nquickly transition to stiff southwesterly flow. Wind gusts of \n35-45 mph are expected during the afternoon hours, and since we \nwon't have great access to rich gulf moisture, we'll be keeping\nan eye on the evolving fire danger as we get closer. As of now,\ntall standing crops remain in place and continue to cure ahead \nof harvest, with saturated soils continuing to dry with the \nwarmer weather through the week. This will be the main fuel \nsources that increase the overall fire danger, with other fuels \ntied to grassland and tree cover still being on the green side \nand still have a ways to go. Nevertheless, dry and windy \nconditions remain the focus for the weekend forecast that \ncontinues into Sunday but with lower wind speeds.\n\nA change comes in the form of a cold front push those \nconditions southward Monday with gusts shifting southeastward \nacross the area. Behind the front, rain chances do creep in but \nwill remain on the lower side until better moisture arrives to \nthe area. We will get good shots of moisture Tuesday and \nWednesday next week as a deep cutoff low develops over the \nwestern CONUS. This low is currently forecast to weaken as it \ntranslates across the Rockies before strengthening as it ejects \nacross the central CONUS. Widespread rain chances will return to\nthe area aided by a strong mass response ahead of the low, but \nrain this time around isn't expected to be the same as the \nrecent tropically-aided rainfall that cause flooding last week. \nWe'll have lower PWATs, less residence time of high-end \nrainfall, and a less direct hit from additional forcing that let\nthe last event reach its heights. Regardless, don't put away \nthe umbrella because next week takes a turn for cooler temps \n(70s) and daily rain chances as that system arrives.",
